   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Economic Burden\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73e3bdda",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Alzheimer’s disease (AD) creates costs for families, caregivers, and the health care system.\n",
    "\n",
    "- In **2025**, about **12 million** caregivers provided **19.2 billion hours** of unpaid care to people with dementia.\n",
    "- Unpaid care for people with AD was valued at **$413.5 billion in 2024**.\n",
    "- Health care, long-term care, and hospice services for people with dementia cost an estimated **$384 billion in 2025**.\n",
    "- Caregiving can also affect caregivers’ physical and mental health.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Source:** [Alzheimer’s Association — Facts and Figures](https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/facts-figures)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "84da7e49",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Risk factors (genetic, lifestyle)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cbf3d473",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Factors associated with a higher risk of Alzheimer’s disease include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Physical health:** Physical inactivity, obesity, high blood pressure, and diabetes.\n",
    "- **Lifestyle:** Smoking and excessive alcohol use.\n",
    "- **Mental and sensory health:** Depression and hearing loss.\n",
    "- **Genetics and demographics:** Family history and sex; women have higher rates of Alzheimer’s.\n",
    "\n",
    "These factors may increase risk, but they do not mean someone will develop Alzheimer’s disease.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Source:** [Alzheimer’s Association — Causes and Risk Factors](https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-alzheimers/causes-and-risk-factors)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "121b0c1f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Societal determinants\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a0f2c683",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Social and environmental conditions can affect brain health and access to dementia care. These include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Socioeconomic factors:** Education, income, and access to health care.\n",
    "- **Neighborhood factors:** Air pollution and access to healthy food.\n",
    "- **Social factors:** Discrimination and other barriers to care.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sources:** [CDC](https://www.cdc.gov/alzheimers-dementia/php/sdoh/index.html) · [DC Brain Health](https://brainhealth.dc.gov/page/social-determinants-health-and-dementia) · [Research article](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12301702/)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c5777f58",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Symptoms\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d541500b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Common symptoms of Alzheimer’s disease include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Memory loss** that disrupts daily life.\n",
    "- **Difficulty planning or solving problems**, such as following familiar tasks or working with numbers.\n",
    "- **Visual and spatial difficulties**, including trouble judging distance or knowing where you are.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Source:** [CDC — Signs and Symptoms of Alzheimer’s Disease](https://www.cdc.gov/alzheimers-dementia/signs-symptoms/alzheimers.html)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2694b19f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Diagnosis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4ce9b91e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Alzheimer’s disease is diagnosed through a medical evaluation. This may include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Medical history and cognitive tests** to assess memory and thinking.\n",
    "- **Physical and neurological exams** to check for other possible causes of symptoms.\n",
    "- **Lab tests and brain scans**, such as MRI, CT, or PET scans.\n",
    "- **Biomarker tests**, including tests of cerebrospinal fluid, when appropriate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Source:** [National Institute on Aging — How Is Alzheimer’s Disease Diagnosed?](https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/alzheimers-symptoms-and-diagnosis/how-alzheimers-disease-diagnosed)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6e56180a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Standard Treatments and Coverage\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8678b4e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Treatment depends on the stage of Alzheimer’s disease. Medicines may help manage symptoms or slow decline; they do not cure the disease.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Treatment | Use | Common risks or side effects |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine** | Help manage symptoms; galantamine and rivastigmine are generally used for mild to moderate disease. Donepezil may be used at different stages. | Nausea, diarrhea, or loss of appetite |\n",
    "| **Memantine** | Helps manage symptoms in moderate to severe disease. | Dizziness, headache, or constipation |\n",
    "| **Lecanemab and donanemab** | For some people with early Alzheimer’s disease; may slow cognitive decline. | Brain swelling or bleeding (ARIA); MRI monitoring is needed |\n",
    "| **Brexpiprazole** | May treat agitation associated with Alzheimer’s disease. | Drowsiness, dizziness, or restlessness |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Coverage:** Insurance coverage and out-of-pocket costs vary by medication and plan. Check with the insurer and health care provider about eligibility and costs.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sources:** [National Institute on Aging — Alzheimer’s Disease Treatment](https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/alzheimers-treatment/how-alzheimers-disease-treated) · [CMS — Medicare Coverage of Alzheimer’s Drugs](https://www.cms.gov/medicare/coverage/evidence/alzheimers-disease)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b6d9c408",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Disease progression & prognosis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a7d98e74",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Alzheimer’s disease progresses differently for each person. On average, people live about **8 years after symptoms begin**, though some live for **20 years or longer**. Early-onset Alzheimer’s is rare and can begin as early as a person’s 30s or 40s.\n",
    "\n",
    "The stages of progression include:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Preclinical stage:** Brain changes begin before noticeable symptoms appear.\n",
    "2. **Early stage:** Mild memory and thinking problems may affect daily activities.\n",
    "3. **Middle stage:** Symptoms worsen, and the person may need more help with daily activities.\n",
    "4. **Late stage:** Severe changes in memory and physical abilities may require full-time care.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Source:** [Johns Hopkins Medicine — Stages of Alzheimer’s Disease](https://www.hopkinsmedicine.org/health/conditions-and-diseases/alzheimers-disease/stages-of-alzheimer-disease)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI Use**: Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "990edb81",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Continuum of Care Providers\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2837a8fb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "People with Alzheimer’s may need different types of support as their needs change. Care options can include:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **In-home services:** Help with daily activities and personal care while living at home.\n",
    "- **Assisted living:** Housing with support for daily activities, meals, and medication routines.\n",
    "- **Memory care:** Specialized support in a setting designed for people with memory conditions.\n",
    "- **Long-term care:** Ongoing assistance for people who need substantial help. It may be provided at home or in a residential facility.\n",
    "\n",
    "Care needs vary, so people may move between services or use more than one type of care.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sources:** [Understanding the Continuum of Care](https://www.edenseniorhc.com/from-independent-living-to-memory-care-understanding-the-continuum-of-care/) · [LCCA — Continuum of Care](https://lcca.com/blog/Continuum-of-Care)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI Use**: Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bbe25da6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Biological mechanisms (anatomy, organ physiology, cell & molecular physiology)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9837c099",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Alzheimer’s disease causes changes in the brain that damage neurons and disrupt their connections. Two key changes are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Amyloid plaques:** Amyloid-beta proteins build up between neurons.\n",
    "- **Tau tangles:** Tau proteins accumulate inside neurons, disrupting their function.\n",
    "\n",
    "As the damage spreads, neurons lose connections and die. This can lead to shrinkage in affected brain areas and problems with memory, thinking, and other abilities. The exact sequence of changes is still being studied.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sources:** [National Institute on Aging — What Happens to the Brain in Alzheimer’s Disease?](https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/alzheimers-causes-and-risk-factors/what-happens-brain-alzheimers-disease) · [YouTube video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YxjFyDUYT5k&t=32s)\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI Use**: Gemini & Google AI helped find credible sources and polish this section. The VS Code agent helped format it.\n"
   ]
  },
